A||N||10||FieroMaster88||Clutch help needed bad!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||FieroMaster88||10-19-2001||01:06 AM||||When we did my engine swap we used the first style master cylinder.  It didn't push my clutch arm far enough to disengage the clutch, so we spaced out the slave cylinder so it would.  <P>This just wasn't working good enough for me, so I broke down and purchased the correct master and a new slave cylinder.  I installed them this evening and was working on bleeding the clutch line.  Well I just don't know what the problem is now.  I have bled the thing over and over again, it just <B>WON'T</B> push the clutch arm far enough.  <P>What is my problem here??????  Is there still air in the clutch line?  What is the best method to bleeding it?  I really need some help with this.  It is pissing me off.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  Thanks for your help.<P>Ooh yeah, some Trans info.  It is an Isuzu 5 speed.  Has the right slave bracket, slave and master.  Also has a new clutch line.    <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                   <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!<p>[This message has been edited by FieroMaster88 (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||RussB||10-19-2001||01:52 AM||||I am in the same position. I have been trying to bleed mine for 2 months. I am braking down and going to replace my after market parts with GM. if anyone has the part numbers for the GM master and slave I would appreciate it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroMaster88||10-19-2001||02:28 AM||||I think I may have just found my problem!  Other than the fact that there is still some air in the lines, I have the crappy aluminum clutch petal!  I am going to try changing that out too, but for now I am going to bleed it the rest of the way (in the morning) and space the slave out towards the clutch lever so I can drive it.  This is my daily driver.  What a pain in the arse!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>Russ, you should try and check out your clutch petal.  Take a magnet to it.  If the magnet is attracted to it you have the steel one, which is the good one.  The aluminum ones bend easily and should be replaced.  Stupid me, should have checked that when I installed the damn thing!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>[EDIT]Does anyone know where I can get a steel clutch petal??? [/EDIT]<p>[This message has been edited by FieroMaster88 (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000003||RussB||10-19-2001||03:15 AM||||I have an 88 which are not suppose to have the alum pedal, however I did stick a magnet to it anyway. <P>I think the best place to get a new pedal is from a Pontiac or Chevy dealer. I think there is a thread in here were somebody posted a part number.<BR>My Fiero was my daily driver too, now my wife is the chauffeur.<P>Good Luck<P>------------------<BR>Black 88 2.5L 5SPD||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||10-19-2001||07:27 AM||||Moving the slave has absolutely no effect on the throw( unless you reach the end of the stroke)and neither does changing the length of the slave push rod. Assuming no leaks or air in the system -throw length is controlled by the stroke of the M/C. Make sure that you have no slop in the pedal linkage, the clutch pedal should rest a little higher than the brake pedal, if it doesn't then I would look for a pedal linkage problem||||reg||1
Z||000005||TooManyFieros||10-19-2001||09:01 AM||||As I said to Russ, many things come into play. If you know what diameter the master cylinder piston is and how far the clutch pedal banjo rod goes into the master (total travel)plus if you know the diameter of the slave piston, you  (or I if you provide the numbers) can do a ratio of volume/travel. Then, you will know how far the slave should move when you push the pedal. This way, if you don't get the travel calculated, you have air in the line. If you have a leak under pressure, oil will end up on the floor pretty quickly. If you are always bleeding air, usually that indicates a faulty slave cyl. So many things come into this. <P>In your case, I wonder about the ratios because you changed so things. <P>As for the alu. pedal, I found that the only alu. was rivited to the pedal and that part is where the banjo connects. Were any of the pedals totally alu? I don't know.<P>Russ, hope you do OK. I still have that 1/2 stick of dynamite. It will fix any and all problems. Ha!||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroBUZZ||10-19-2001||09:19 AM||||I don't know if this is any help or not.  If you have tried everything else, maybe so. A friend who is an old time mechanic and into stock cars says they do their hydraulic clutch this way (mostly due to space probs).<BR>He uses a pump, I don't know the real name, but it's a canister that you raise the arm and as it lowers, it forces the oil/fluid in it out). The fluid is forced thru the bleeder on the slave (s/b lower than the master in height) and as it fills the system it forces the air out thru the master reservoir.  When the master is full it should be fine.<BR>I've seen him do it on a 36 Chevy stock car and a little 750cc Kawi demon car. It seems to work okay.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000007||RussB||11-02-2001||01:05 PM||||Is it fixed yet. was the pedal the problem.<P>------------------<BR>Black 88 2.5L 5SPD||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroMaster88||11-02-2001||01:47 PM||||Well, Yesterday I replaced all my clutch parts with GM parts and bent my petal back until I can find a new one...I have to finish bleeding it today and we will see.  I sure hope it is fixed!||||reg||1
